  "text": "A general refused to go to war unless she came with him. She went. Then told him he had just lost the credit. Judges chapter four. Deborah. Prophetess. And the text says it flat: she judged Israel at that time. Not helped. Not advised. Judged. She holds court under a palm tree between Ramah and Bethel, and the children of Israel come up to her for judgment. That is the office. Written down. No hedging. So she summons Barak. Military commander. And hands him God's orders. Take ten thousand men to mount Tabor. And Barak, a grown general, says: \"If thou wilt go with me, then I will go. But if thou wilt not go with me, then I will not go.\" Sir. She goes. Of course she goes. But she calls it first. \"The journey that thou takest shall not be for thine honour, for the Lord shall sell Sisera into the hand of a woman.\" She told him exactly how it would end before it started. And that is exactly how it ended. In a tent. With a woman named Jael and a hammer. So the record reads: the prophetess gave the orders, the general needed a chaperone, and another woman finished it. Palm tree. Entire nation.",
